## Communication Shortcuts

### Reference Points
When presenting 3+ findings, decisions, options, risks, questions, or actions, assign each a short stable code and keep it stable for the whole conversation: `D1..` decisions, `O1..` options, `F1..` findings, `R1..` risks, `Q1..` questions, `A1..` actions. Invent new prefixes for new categories. Skip codes for short simple answers. The user can then say "expand R2" or "skip D1, do A3".
- Exception, tracker items: never invent a code (`T1..` or any other) for tickets/issues. Before publishing, a draft list uses plain list numbers only. Once published, an issue is referred to only by its own tracker number (`#12`) in chat, titles, bodies and "Blocked by" lines; issue titles carry no code prefix. Reason: invented codes drift from the real issue numbers.

### Evidence Before Completion Claims
Never claim work is complete without evidence: show the test output, the diff, or the file path that proves it. "Done" without proof is forbidden.

## Personal Preferences

### Language
- Talk to {{USER_NAME}} in {{LANGUAGE}}.
- Sub-agent briefs and prompts: English.
- Documents people read are {{LANGUAGE}}: issues, specs, tickets, ADRs, CONTEXT.md, notes/GOALS/BACKLOG/DONE, `reports/`, PR bodies, vault notes. Commit messages (after the `feat:`-style prefix) and code comments: {{LANGUAGE}}. Code identifiers and technical terms stay as they are.


### Technical Glossary
- Maintain `~/.claude/GLOSSARY.md`: every technical term used when talking to the user (gate, CAS, worktree, fail-closed, ...) must have a one-line plain-{{LANGUAGE}} definition there.
- When a session introduces a technical term not yet in the glossary, append it (same format: `- **term** — short plain-language definition`) before the session ends.
- On first use of a rare/new term in conversation, give the one-line meaning inline; the glossary is the durable reference the user can open anytime.

### Privacy
- Always redact logs; never share secrets (API keys/tokens/passwords/JWTs)
- Review output before sharing — remove any sensitive data

### Code Style
- No emojis in code, comments, or documentation (unless user explicitly asks)
- Prefer immutability — never mutate objects or arrays
- Many small files over few large files
- 200-400 lines typical, 800 max per file

### Git
- Conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`, `test:`
- Always test locally before committing
- Small, focused commits

### Testing
- TDD: write tests first
- 80% minimum coverage
- Unit + integration + E2E for critical flows
- These hold when `mattpocock-skills:tdd` runs too: it sets no coverage target, this rule does (decision 2026-09-10).

### Knowledge Capture
- Personal debugging notes, preferences, temporary context → auto memory
- Team/project knowledge (architecture decisions, API changes, runbooks) → follow the project's existing docs structure
- If the current task already produces the relevant docs/comments/examples, don't duplicate the same knowledge elsewhere
- If there's no obvious project doc location, ask before creating a new top-level doc

### Research Reports
- Any substantive research/investigation done on request (external doc analysis, codebase audits, tool evaluations) → write the full findings to `<projectRoot>/reports/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md` before finishing the task, so future agents re-read the report instead of re-gathering context (token savings).
- Include: source, date, key findings, decisions taken (adopted/rejected and why). {{LANGUAGE}} (see Language).
- Check `reports/` before starting new research on a topic — a prior report may already cover it.

### Docs Fetching
- Mintlify-hosted docs sites (e.g. docs.warp.dev) serve a raw markdown twin of every page at `<page-url>.md` and a full index at `<site>/llms.txt`. Fetch the `.md` variant — fetching the HTML URL summarizes and loses detail. Check for this pattern on any docs site before deep research.

### Project bootstrap
- New code project, or existing one missing the skeleton → load skill `proje-baslat` (creates CLAUDE.md, notes.md, GOALS.md, BACKLOG.md, DONE.md, CONSTANTS.md, CONTEXT.md, docs/adr/, BAGIMLILIKLAR.md, reports/, references/, git + GitHub, then mattpocock-skills setup with GitHub Issues). Not inside the vault.
- `BAGIMLILIKLAR.md` = things that break silently when something else changes, each with a check command. A global SessionStart hook runs the doctor for you (skeleton check live, full registry from a background cache) and injects text only when red; act on that text. Run `python ~/.claude/skills/proje-baslat/doktor.py <root>` by hand after infra/config/name changes (red `iskelet` = older template; repair = `proje-baslat` retrofit mode); harness registry is `~/.claude/BAGIMLILIKLAR.md`. On red, do the `bozuksa:` action; never edit the check to make it green. Adding entries is YOUR job, not the user's: in the same change that couples two things (same value in two places, a script/job/backup naming a resource, an env var or secret name, an external endpoint/webhook/DNS record, a local patch an update will overwrite), add the entry with a working `kontrol:` command, run the doctor once, and mention it in one line. Do not ask permission to add. Couplings outside the repo are invisible to you; ask about them once when deployment or integration comes up.
- `BACKLOG.md` = started-and-unfinished work. Mandatory: before stopping mid-task, append what was done, what remains, where. Read it at session start. Finished items move to `DONE.md`.
- `CONSTANTS.md` = never-changing values. When unsure about an address, port, name or ID, read it; never guess.
- Planned project work = GitHub Issues (`docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`). Project-less personal tasks → `~/.claude/TODO.md`; never route project items there.

